# PHASE 7 — Graph & Config Storage Model Rework

**Read `00 - START HERE` and finish Phase 6 first** — this phase normalizes the config shape Phase 6 just built, so doing it first would mean redoing this once Phase 6 lands anyway.

This is still `localStorage` under a Zustand `persist` middleware — there is no real database in this phase, no API calls, no backend. What changes is the *shape* of what's stored, so that (a) the questions you actually asked about — "how many bots use model X," "how many nodes use tool Y," "which components are usually wired together" — become real, fast, working features instead of impossible ones, and (b) migrating to a real relational database later is a matter of copying this shape into real tables, not redesigning it from scratch.

---

## 1. What's wrong with today's shape

`useWorkspace` (`lib/workspace-store.ts`) holds one `bots: Bot[]` array. Each `Bot` embeds its own `nodes: BotNode[]` and `edges: BotEdge[]` directly. This is fine for "load one bot, edit its graph, save it back" — which is all the app does today — but it structurally cannot answer any question that spans *across* bots, because there's no single place where "all nodes, from every bot, in one list" exists. To count how many bots use a given component, you'd have to loop every bot and every node inside it, every single time, with no way to cache or index it. That's the JSONB-blob problem you correctly flagged wanting to move away from — and the fix doesn't require a real database to start, it requires normalizing the *local* shape first.

---

## 2. The new normalized shape

Replace the single `bots: Bot[]` array with flat, id-keyed tables plus join tables — this is deliberately shaped like what the eventual Postgres schema will look like (see §7's appendix), so that later work is a lift-and-shift, not a rewrite.

```
interface WorkspaceState {
  // Metadata only — no nodes/edges embedded anymore.
  botsById: Record<string, BotMeta>
  botOrder: string[]                              // preserves list ordering without relying on object key order

  nodesById: Record<string, BotNode & { botId: string }>
  edgesById: Record<string, BotEdge & { botId: string }>
  graphIndex: Record<string, { nodeIds: string[]; edgeIds: string[] }>   // botId -> its node/edge ids

  versionsById: Record<string, BotVersion & { botId: string; nodeIds: string[]; edgeIds: string[] }>
  runsById: Record<string, BacktestRun>

  // presets, notifications, etc. — same normalization treatment, lower priority, do bots/nodes/edges first
  ...
}

interface BotMeta {
  id: string
  name: string
  description: string
  status: BotStatus
  createdAt: string
  updatedAt: string
  tags: string[]
  visibility: 'private' | 'unlisted' | 'public'
  headlineMetric: { label: string; value: string; positive: boolean }
  runIds: string[]
  versionIds: string[]
  notes?: CanvasNote[]
  frames?: CanvasFrame[]
}
```

Node and edge records gain a `botId` foreign key so a flat scan (`Object.values(nodesById)`) is meaningful on its own — this is the one field that makes the whole normalization pay off.

