## 4. Rewrite the write paths

`saveGraph(botId, nodes, edges, extra)` (called from the builder page's manual save, autosave, and preset-fork flows) currently does `bot.nodes = nodes; bot.edges = edges` in effect. Rewrite it to upsert into the flat tables and rebuild that bot's `graphIndex` entry:

```
saveGraph: (botId, nodes, edges, extra) => set((state) => {
  const nodesById = { ...state.nodesById }
  const edgesById = { ...state.edgesById }

  // Remove this bot's previous node/edge ids before re-adding the current set,
  // so a deleted node doesn't linger as an orphaned record.
  const prevIdx = state.graphIndex[botId]
  if (prevIdx) {
    for (const id of prevIdx.nodeIds) delete nodesById[id]
    for (const id of prevIdx.edgeIds) delete edgesById[id]
  }
  for (const n of nodes) nodesById[n.id] = { ...n, botId }
  for (const e of edges) edgesById[e.id] = { ...e, botId }

  return {
    nodesById,
    edgesById,
    graphIndex: {
      ...state.graphIndex,
      [botId]: { nodeIds: nodes.map((n) => n.id), edgeIds: edges.map((e) => e.id) },
    },
    botsById: {
      ...state.botsById,
      [botId]: { ...state.botsById[botId], updatedAt: new Date().toISOString(), ...extra },
    },
  }
}),
```

Apply the equivalent treatment to `createBot`, `forkPreset`, `deleteBot` (must also delete the bot's node/edge/version/run records, not just its `botsById` entry — an orphaned-record cleanup this normalization now makes your responsibility, whereas before deleting the array element deleted everything nested inside it for free), and `snapshotVersion` (store the version's own `nodeIds`/`edgeIds` referencing the *same* node/edge records if nothing changed since the last snapshot, or its own copies if the graph diverged — simplest correct approach: always snapshot a full copy into `versionsById` with its own fresh ids, accept the storage duplication, don't try to build a real diff/delta system for this).

