## 7. Appendix: the real schema this maps onto, for whenever a backend gets built

Not part of this phase's deliverable — no backend work happens here — but worth writing down now while the shape is fresh, since it's the natural next artifact after this phase and answers the "how would this become relational tables" question directly:

```sql
create table bots (
  id            uuid primary key,
  user_id       uuid references users(id),
  name          text not null,
  description   text,
  status        text not null,
  visibility    text not null default 'private',
  tags          text[] not null default '{}',
  created_at    timestamptz not null default now(),
  updated_at    timestamptz not null default now()
);

create table nodes (
  id            uuid primary key,
  bot_id        uuid references bots(id) on delete cascade,
  component_id  text not null,       -- foreign key to a real `components` catalog table, not shown here
  x             real not null,
  y             real not null,
  enabled       boolean not null default true,
  config        jsonb not null default '{}'   -- the FieldDef-keyed config object from Phase 6, unchanged
);

create table edges (
  id            uuid primary key,
  bot_id        uuid references bots(id) on delete cascade,
  source_node   uuid references nodes(id) on delete cascade,
  target_node   uuid references nodes(id) on delete cascade
);

create table bot_versions (
  id            uuid primary key,
  bot_id        uuid references bots(id) on delete cascade,
  label         text,
  note          text,
  created_at    timestamptz not null default now()
);
-- version_nodes / version_edges as their own snapshot tables, same shape as nodes/edges above,
-- foreign-keyed to bot_versions instead of bots directly.

create table backtest_runs (
  id            uuid primary key,
  bot_id        uuid references bots(id),
  config        jsonb not null,
  metrics       jsonb not null,
  created_at    timestamptz not null default now()
);

-- materialized view or scheduled query, directly answering "how many bots use component X":
create materialized view component_usage as
  select component_id, count(distinct bot_id) as bot_count, count(*) as node_count
  from nodes
  group by component_id;
```
`config jsonb` staying as a JSON column rather than being fully normalized into its own columns is deliberate and correct even in a real database — `FieldDef`-shaped config is heterogeneous per component by design (Phase 6 made that heterogeneity a feature, not a bug), and Postgres's `jsonb` supports indexing and querying into it directly (`config -> 'model' ->> 'providerId'`) well enough that you don't need a fully relational config schema. What you *do* want relational, and what this phase already gives you locally, are the node/edge/bot boundaries themselves — that's what makes cross-bot queries fast in a real database the same way `nodesById` makes them possible locally.

